1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,071
Today we've chosen a title for our talk

2
00:00:02,071 --> 00:00:04,000
namely, "The Flood Returns."

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,000
I wonder what this title brings to your
mind?

4
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,142
Perhaps some of you will think back to

5
00:00:09,142 --> 00:00:12,280
a few years ago, when National Geographic

6
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:14,123
once produced a well-known documentary

7
00:00:14,123 --> 00:00:15,916
in English it was called Before the Flood

8
00:00:15,916 --> 00:00:20,000
and in Chinese it was given the title
"Before the Flood Comes."

9
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
It, too, was all about paying attention to
climate change.

10
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,019
Now, today is not a movie-appreciation
session

11
00:00:27,019 --> 00:00:32,100
but you can actually find that documentary
on YouTube if you'd like to watch it.

12
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:35,420
Choosing this title today is really quite
fitting.

13
00:00:35,420 --> 00:00:39,893
Why is that? Because, as you know, just
two or three weeks ago

14
00:00:39,893 --> 00:00:43,620
there was a climate summit held over in
the United Kingdom.

15
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:48,220
I imagine none of us went there to join
the demonstrations outside.

16
00:00:48,220 --> 00:00:50,314
There was also a recent survey

17
00:00:50,314 --> 00:00:53,590
which showed that three-quarters of all
young people

18
00:00:53,590 --> 00:00:56,920
are absolutely filled with fear about the
future.

19
00:00:56,920 --> 00:00:59,480
And the reason for that is climate change.

20
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:01,918
So many young people today

21
00:01:01,918 --> 00:01:09,000
feel utterly lost about what lies ahead,
because the road before them seems so
uncertain.

22
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:12,219
The flood is coming.

23
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:16,895
I remember, back in 2009, there was a news
story

24
00:01:16,895 --> 00:01:22,260
about the Indian Ocean island nation of
the Maldives.

25
00:01:22,260 --> 00:01:26,257
Their cabinet held a meeting, and they
deliberately went down to the seabed to do
it

26
00:01:26,257 --> 00:01:31,220
that's right, they put on diving gear and
held the entire cabinet meeting
underwater.

27
00:01:31,220 --> 00:01:32,553
Why would they ever do that?

28
00:01:32,553 --> 00:01:37,620
They wanted to draw the attention of the
whole world, the attention of the entire
international community.

29
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:42,199
Their message was: if we ignore climate
change and the sea keeps rising like this

30
00:01:42,199 --> 00:01:45,560
then their whole country will one day end
up beneath the sea.

31
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:47,882
And over here, this is the Solomon Islands

32
00:01:47,882 --> 00:01:52,120
the Solomon Islands, located in the
Pacific, in the South Pacific.

33
00:01:52,740 --> 00:01:56,920
This clip comes from the current-affairs
programme 60 Minutes.

34
00:01:57,100 --> 00:01:59,886
The programme interviewed one of the local
residents living there.

35
00:01:59,886 --> 00:02:01,531
In the picture at the very top

36
00:02:01,531 --> 00:02:02,816
what you are looking at

37
00:02:02,816 --> 00:02:03,536
is this man

38
00:02:03,536 --> 00:02:05,541
who, back when he was a child

39
00:02:05,541 --> 00:02:05,901
would

40
00:02:05,901 --> 00:02:07,340
often come here to play.

41
00:02:07,340 --> 00:02:08,037
But now

42
00:02:08,037 --> 00:02:09,720
this island is completely gone.

43
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:11,940
You can no longer see the island there at
all.

44
00:02:11,940 --> 00:02:16,040
The sea keeps rising, and a great deal of
land has already been swallowed up.

45
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:18,007
And by the way, over on your

46
00:02:18,007 --> 00:02:19,918
picture in the bottom-right corner

47
00:02:19,918 --> 00:02:21,660
is also quite interesting.

48
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:23,036
Because as the sea rises

49
00:02:23,036 --> 00:02:26,500
the plants that once grew there, since
seawater is salty

50
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:28,320
those plants begin to die off.

51
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:29,139
This is a point

52
00:02:29,139 --> 00:02:31,640
that we'll come back to again a little
later.

53
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:35,517
As the sea slowly rises, more and more
land ends up submerged.

54
00:02:35,517 --> 00:02:38,384
And you'll notice it isn't only the island
nations affected.

55
00:02:38,384 --> 00:02:40,860
This here is Venice, in Italy.

56
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:42,570
Because the sea level keeps rising

57
00:02:42,570 --> 00:02:44,974
in recent years it has again and again

58
00:02:44,974 --> 00:02:46,500
suffered from flooding.

59
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:49,367
I wonder if you've ever heard of the
so-called "MOSE Project"?

60
00:02:49,367 --> 00:02:53,400
They planned to build a whole set of
floodgates to hold back the seawater.

61
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:56,480
This was the flooding as it looked back in
2019.

62
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:58,780
That bridge in the background is really
quite famous.

63
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:00,823
If you've ever been to the Venetian hotel

64
00:03:00,823 --> 00:03:03,640
there's a bridge just like it outside,
isn't there?

65
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:05,780
This spot here was originally a canal.

66
00:03:05,780 --> 00:03:08,451
But the seawater came flooding in, all the
way up to

67
00:03:08,451 --> 00:03:12,340
the shops and the pavements, and you can
see the boats sitting right here.

68
00:03:12,340 --> 00:03:16,297
And it wasn't only 2019, it flooded all
over again in 2020.

69
00:03:16,297 --> 00:03:18,842
In other words, in recent years it floods
over and over.

70
00:03:18,842 --> 00:03:20,290
Here in New Zealand,

71
00:03:20,290 --> 00:03:22,968
I don't know if you've followed this,

72
00:03:22,968 --> 00:03:26,731
but up above you there's a country called
Indonesia,

73
00:03:26,731 --> 00:03:29,265
and Indonesia's capital is Jakarta,

74
00:03:29,265 --> 00:03:32,811
which recently announced it's moving the
capital.

75
00:03:32,811 --> 00:03:33,680
Why is that?

76
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:36,633
Because Jakarta sits right by the sea,

77
00:03:36,633 --> 00:03:40,053
and as the waters rise it floods
constantly;

78
00:03:40,053 --> 00:03:41,607
every time it rains,

79
00:03:41,607 --> 00:03:43,784
every time a storm blows in,

80
00:03:43,784 --> 00:03:44,561
it floods.

81
00:03:44,561 --> 00:03:49,224
The reason is that so many people there
pump up groundwater,

82
00:03:49,224 --> 00:03:53,887
which has caused Jakarta's land to subside
and keep sinking;

83
00:03:53,887 --> 00:03:55,986
add to that the rising sea,

84
00:03:55,986 --> 00:03:59,095
and every year the flooding grows worse,

85
00:03:59,095 --> 00:04:03,680
so they've decided to relocate to the
island of Kalimantan.

86
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:07,802
This is actually affecting many countries
and a great many people.

87
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:10,658
For example, over in Bangladesh

88
00:04:10,658 --> 00:04:12,807
many people along the coast have already
moved away

89
00:04:12,807 --> 00:04:15,200
to areas further north and further inland.

90
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:16,662
These people are what we call

91
00:04:16,662 --> 00:04:18,280
climate refugees.

92
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:21,299
And it's all because the sea keeps on
rising.

93
00:04:21,860 --> 00:04:23,879
But it isn't only the rising of the sea.

94
00:04:23,879 --> 00:04:27,271
This scene here, if you follow the news,
you'll surely recognise.

95
00:04:27,271 --> 00:04:29,182
This July, in Henan, in China

96
00:04:29,182 --> 00:04:31,142
because of relentless torrential rain

97
00:04:31,142 --> 00:04:36,320
you saw scenes exactly like this: the
tunnels flooded, and the casualties were
severe.

98
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:41,196
The picture in the lower left is the
subway line that was flooded that very day

99
00:04:41,196 --> 00:04:46,479
and inside the subway carriages, the water
rose right up to people's chests.

100
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:51,820
So why does so much rain fall, and why
does it flood this badly?

101
00:04:51,820 --> 00:04:55,261
It's because, as the climate warms and the
air heats up

102
00:04:55,261 --> 00:04:58,856
the amount of moisture the air can hold
becomes much greater

103
00:04:58,856 --> 00:05:03,221
and so these bouts of torrential rain,
these sudden cloudbursts

104
00:05:03,221 --> 00:05:06,200
become extremely severe the moment they
hit.

105
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:09,683
In just a few hours you can get several
days' worth of rain

106
00:05:09,683 --> 00:05:12,934
and in just a few days, a whole year's
worth of rain.

107
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:15,680
Do you recognise where this place is?

108
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:16,700
Probably not, I'd guess.

109
00:05:16,700 --> 00:05:18,674
This, too, was in the month of July

110
00:05:18,674 --> 00:05:20,705
but this one is over in New Zealand.

111
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:23,635
It rained, and after just a single weekend

112
00:05:23,635 --> 00:05:26,680
this was the scene once the rain had
finally stopped.

113
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:28,096
So it isn't only China

114
00:05:28,096 --> 00:05:30,078
and it isn't only the Northern Hemisphere

115
00:05:30,078 --> 00:05:32,580
the Southern Hemisphere is exactly the
same.

116
00:05:32,580 --> 00:05:33,706
Just a few days ago

117
00:05:33,706 --> 00:05:38,209
if you follow the news, you'll know
Vancouver was hit by severe rainfall

118
00:05:38,209 --> 00:05:42,820
which cut off all the transport links
between Vancouver and the surrounding
areas.

119
00:05:42,820 --> 00:05:45,260
Because so many of the highways were
flooded

120
00:05:45,260 --> 00:05:46,313
and many of the bridges

121
00:05:46,313 --> 00:05:47,940
were simply washed away.

122
00:05:47,940 --> 00:05:49,403
If you look over to the right

123
00:05:49,403 --> 00:05:51,493
that there is the railway track

124
00:05:51,493 --> 00:05:52,486
the railway line

125
00:05:52,486 --> 00:05:55,360
and it, too, was destroyed.

126
00:05:55,360 --> 00:06:00,004
And you'll find it isn't only cloudbursts
that cause this kind of damage.

127
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:07,360
In recent years, if you pay attention,
every time a storm comes you'll hear this
term, the "storm surge."

128
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:11,064
A storm surge means this: at the time when
a storm blows in

129
00:06:11,064 --> 00:06:13,434
the eye of the storm is an area of low
pressure

130
00:06:13,434 --> 00:06:15,360
which pulls the seawater upward.

131
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:19,360
So every time a storm hits, the tide rises
higher and higher.

132
00:06:19,360 --> 00:06:21,360
And that is what we call a storm surge.

133
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:25,365
This was back in 2005, over in the United
States

134
00:06:25,365 --> 00:06:29,951
the scene after the rising sea came and
struck New Orleans.

135
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:31,821
So it really doesn't matter what your

136
00:06:31,821 --> 00:06:33,679
particular city happens to be like

137
00:06:33,679 --> 00:06:35,854
even a country like the United States

138
00:06:35,854 --> 00:06:37,440
you'll find it floods just the same.

139
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:39,297
As I mentioned just a moment ago

140
00:06:39,297 --> 00:06:43,320
rising seawater causes the plants along
the coast to die.

141
00:06:43,320 --> 00:06:49,720
In fact, people later studied why Katrina
caused such catastrophic flooding in New
Orleans.

142
00:06:49,720 --> 00:06:52,564
On the one hand, of course, it was Katrina
itself

143
00:06:52,564 --> 00:06:54,270
the winds were especially strong

144
00:06:54,270 --> 00:06:56,640
and on top of that, some of the levees
broke.

145
00:06:56,640 --> 00:07:00,625
But there was also one very important
reason, and it is this:

146
00:07:00,625 --> 00:07:02,023
as the sea kept on rising

147
00:07:02,023 --> 00:07:05,231
it killed off the plants in many of the
coastal marshlands

148
00:07:05,231 --> 00:07:06,784
so that along the coastline

149
00:07:06,784 --> 00:07:10,200
there was no longer any buffer zone left
to protect it.

150
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:12,415
So when the storm finally came

151
00:07:12,415 --> 00:07:15,184
the winds, and the great sea waves

152
00:07:15,184 --> 00:07:17,620
struck the city directly.

153
00:07:17,620 --> 00:07:18,387
This here was

154
00:07:18,387 --> 00:07:21,335
the situation with Katrina back in 2005.

155
00:07:21,335 --> 00:07:24,934
And this is Hurricane Sandy, in 2012.

156
00:07:25,040 --> 00:07:28,987
I don't know if you recognise where this
is, but this is New York.

157
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:31,700
right on the east coast of the United
States.

158
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:37,515
And it's worth pointing out that New York
actually lies at over 40 degrees north
latitude.

159
00:07:37,515 --> 00:07:39,817
The word "hurricane" simply means a great
wind.

160
00:07:39,817 --> 00:07:42,684
If it were in Hong Kong, we would call it
a "typhoon."

161
00:07:42,684 --> 00:07:46,040
Its proper scientific name is actually the
"tropical cyclone."

162
00:07:46,040 --> 00:07:48,860
that is to say, a cyclone of the tropics.

163
00:07:48,860 --> 00:07:53,860
As the name itself suggests, these storms
all form in the tropical regions.

164
00:07:53,860 --> 00:07:56,860
Yet New York sits at over forty degrees
north.

165
00:07:56,860 --> 00:08:02,004
So you can see how global warming is now
driving many tropical disasters

166
00:08:02,004 --> 00:08:07,860
such as storms striking far to the north,
and it seems to be becoming a new normal.

167
00:08:07,860 --> 00:08:11,860
Likewise, if you've ever been to New York,
you'll know the lay of the land there.

168
00:08:11,860 --> 00:08:14,646
The rising sea, together with the storm
surge

169
00:08:14,646 --> 00:08:18,187
flooded Manhattan, specifically Lower
Manhattan.

170
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:22,416
You know, New York actually has a nickname

171
00:08:22,416 --> 00:08:24,680
the "City that Never Sleeps."

172
00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:30,276
Lower Manhattan is basically a financial
centre

173
00:08:30,276 --> 00:08:33,661
lit up around the clock, twenty-four hours
a day

174
00:08:33,661 --> 00:08:34,806
because of all of the

175
00:08:34,806 --> 00:08:36,100
financial trading.

176
00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:40,098
As it turns out, many financial
institutions keep their computers down
underground.

177
00:08:40,098 --> 00:08:41,159
And that particular flood

178
00:08:41,159 --> 00:08:42,260
caused very serious damage.

179
00:08:42,260 --> 00:08:46,620
In fact, it took several years afterward
to slowly recover from it.

180
00:08:46,620 --> 00:08:49,174
The subways were flooded in exactly the
same way

181
00:08:49,174 --> 00:08:51,206
and swarms of rats came scurrying out

182
00:08:51,206 --> 00:08:55,153
as if giving the whole city a thorough
washing.

183
00:08:56,340 --> 00:08:57,711
In this picture on your right

184
00:08:57,711 --> 00:09:02,740
it's hard to imagine a subway station,
usually so busy with people, completely
underwater.

185
00:09:02,740 --> 00:09:05,560
You can see the divers there carrying out
the search and rescue.

186
00:09:05,560 --> 00:09:10,668
This is Macau, back in 2017, struck by the
typhoon Hato.

187
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:15,754
Because of this storm surge, the seawater
came pouring back inland

188
00:09:15,754 --> 00:09:17,840
leaving the whole city in this state.

189
00:09:17,840 --> 00:09:20,061
And here, too, the casualties were severe.

190
00:09:20,061 --> 00:09:23,709
I didn't actually put up any of the photos
from Hong Kong

191
00:09:23,709 --> 00:09:25,367
but you'll notice that every single year

192
00:09:25,367 --> 00:09:26,238
whenever a storm comes

193
00:09:26,238 --> 00:09:30,673
in recent years, Hato in 2017, and
Mangkhut in 2018

194
00:09:30,673 --> 00:09:33,617
you'll find the coastal areas, whether
it's Tseung Kwan O

195
00:09:33,617 --> 00:09:36,394
or Heng Fa Chuen and other low-lying spots

196
00:09:36,394 --> 00:09:38,840
the coastline gets battered by the waves
again and again.

197
00:09:38,840 --> 00:09:39,922
And speaking of all this

198
00:09:39,922 --> 00:09:43,120
I'm reminded of a verse in the Bible, in
Luke chapter 21.

199
00:09:43,120 --> 00:09:47,300
The Bible describes the state of the world
before the Lord returns

200
00:09:47,300 --> 00:09:50,144
and among those words is this one line:

201
00:09:50,144 --> 00:09:52,060
on the earth distress of nations

202
00:09:52,060 --> 00:09:57,110
in perplexity because of the roaring of
the sea and the waves.

203
00:09:58,120 --> 00:10:01,545
The Bible also speaks about our last days

204
00:10:01,545 --> 00:10:06,421
and one of the disasters it names seems to
be the very waves of the sea.

205
00:10:07,120 --> 00:10:11,220
We've just been looking at this rising of
the sea,

206
00:10:11,220 --> 00:10:12,533
at storm surges;

207
00:10:12,533 --> 00:10:13,517
and in fact,

208
00:10:13,517 --> 00:10:16,715
I first noticed this verse back in 2004

209
00:10:16,715 --> 00:10:20,440
and that was because of the great South
Asian tsunami.

210
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:25,811
And of course there was also the tsunami
triggered by the great East Japan
earthquake in 2011.

211
00:10:25,811 --> 00:10:27,929
These events all seem to be telling us
something

212
00:10:27,929 --> 00:10:30,862
this very situation, that because of the
waves of the sea

213
00:10:30,862 --> 00:10:32,980
people are gripped with fear and
confusion.

214
00:10:32,980 --> 00:10:37,624
And this reminds me of something the Bible
records in particular.

215
00:10:37,980 --> 00:10:42,980
When the Lord Jesus was on earth, He once
helped His disciples out on the Sea of
Galilee.

216
00:10:42,980 --> 00:10:49,320
They suddenly ran into a fierce storm;
now, some of the disciples were fishermen
and had seen storms before

217
00:10:49,320 --> 00:10:53,197
but the storm was so violent that they
even thought they were about to die.

218
00:10:53,197 --> 00:10:58,980
Yet the Lord was there asleep in the
stern; they woke Him, crying out, "Lord,
we're perishing!"

219
00:10:58,980 --> 00:11:04,100
The Lord got up, rebuked the wind and the
waves, and made the whole storm completely
calm.

220
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:07,180
I find this account recorded in the Bible
deeply meaningful.

221
00:11:07,180 --> 00:11:09,154
On the one hand, you see the Lord Jesus

222
00:11:09,154 --> 00:11:12,288
He truly is able to rescue us out of every
danger.

223
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:16,900
And on the other, it shows that the Lord
can calm any storm.

224
00:11:16,900 --> 00:11:19,107
No matter what it is, in your own life

225
00:11:19,107 --> 00:11:20,608
whatever hardship you happen to meet

226
00:11:20,608 --> 00:11:23,300
whatever blows come, whatever storms arise

227
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:25,860
the Lord is able to calm every single
storm.

228
00:11:25,860 --> 00:11:29,633
If a Christian friend has invited you here
today

229
00:11:29,633 --> 00:11:30,565
the truth is,

230
00:11:30,565 --> 00:11:35,083
your Christian friends would be very glad
to share with you how

231
00:11:35,083 --> 00:11:39,386
the Lord has helped them calm the storms
in their own lives.

232
00:11:42,860 --> 00:11:45,000
Alright, let me pose a question to
everyone.

233
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:51,860
In this glass here, it's filled up with
ice, and filled with water too, right up
to the very brim.

234
00:11:51,860 --> 00:11:52,919
After a while,

235
00:11:52,919 --> 00:11:54,734
once the ice has melted,

236
00:11:54,734 --> 00:11:57,154
here is my question for you all:

237
00:11:57,154 --> 00:11:59,726
will the water overflow the glass,

238
00:11:59,726 --> 00:12:00,860
or will it not?

239
00:12:00,860 --> 00:12:01,615
It won't.

240
00:12:02,860 --> 00:12:05,530
It won't, will it? Very impressive indeed.

241
00:12:06,860 --> 00:12:08,851
So what am I really getting at?

242
00:12:08,851 --> 00:12:10,777
Water is a very special thing,

243
00:12:10,777 --> 00:12:12,704
and not just in its structure,

244
00:12:12,704 --> 00:12:13,860
because it is H₂O;

245
00:12:13,860 --> 00:12:15,016
the H is hydrogen,

246
00:12:15,016 --> 00:12:15,401
which,

247
00:12:15,401 --> 00:12:16,172
as you know,

248
00:12:16,172 --> 00:12:17,456
is itself flammable,

249
00:12:17,456 --> 00:12:18,741
and the O is oxygen,

250
00:12:18,741 --> 00:12:20,860
which itself supports combustion.

251
00:12:20,860 --> 00:12:22,193
And yet, remarkably

252
00:12:22,193 --> 00:12:26,917
hydrogen combined together with oxygen
becomes water, and water is able to put
out fire.

253
00:12:26,917 --> 00:12:31,359
There's another special thing too:
everything expands with heat and contracts
with cold.

254
00:12:31,359 --> 00:12:35,200
The lower the temperature falls, the more
the volume of water shrinks down.

255
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:36,883
But here is the strange part.

256
00:12:36,980 --> 00:12:38,957
When it reaches 4 degrees Celsius

257
00:12:38,957 --> 00:12:39,685
its volume

258
00:12:39,685 --> 00:12:42,027
shrinks right down to its smallest point

259
00:12:42,027 --> 00:12:44,420
and then it shrinks no further at all.

260
00:12:44,420 --> 00:12:49,780
At 3 degrees, then 2, then 1, and at 0
degrees when it freezes, the volume
actually starts to expand.

261
00:12:49,780 --> 00:12:53,727
And so its density, instead of rising,
decreases.

262
00:12:53,880 --> 00:12:58,480
And because the density is now lower,
you'll notice the ice floats on top of the
water.

263
00:12:58,480 --> 00:13:01,145
So you see, ice really is quite a special
thing.

264
00:13:01,145 --> 00:13:04,048
And why is that? I believe it is the mercy
of God.

265
00:13:04,880 --> 00:13:09,403
Just think about it, if water became
denser as it froze and sank down to the
bottom

266
00:13:09,403 --> 00:13:12,593
then every single living creature in the
sea would die.

267
00:13:12,593 --> 00:13:18,700
Just imagine it, it would be like a person
walking to the edge of a cliff, and the
moment they fall off, they're dead.

268
00:13:18,700 --> 00:13:21,172
But you'll find it's a wonderful thing:

269
00:13:21,172 --> 00:13:22,376
when water freezes,

270
00:13:22,376 --> 00:13:24,658
its volume actually expands instead,

271
00:13:24,658 --> 00:13:25,799
its density drops,

272
00:13:25,799 --> 00:13:27,700
and so it floats on the water.

273
00:13:27,700 --> 00:13:32,707
There's one more special thing too. What
is it? You can see the ice looks quite
large, can't you?

274
00:13:32,707 --> 00:13:35,649
But when the ice turns back into water,

275
00:13:35,649 --> 00:13:37,761
its volume actually shrinks;

276
00:13:37,761 --> 00:13:38,364
because,

277
00:13:38,364 --> 00:13:39,118
as I said,

278
00:13:39,118 --> 00:13:41,004
freezing makes it bigger,

279
00:13:41,004 --> 00:13:44,700
and turning back to water makes it smaller
again.

280
00:13:44,700 --> 00:13:51,318
And the volume it loses is precisely the
part that had been sticking out above the
water.

281
00:13:51,620 --> 00:13:55,214
So some people say, when you see an
iceberg, you'd better get well away from
it.

282
00:13:55,214 --> 00:13:59,656
In fact, the part of an iceberg sticking
out above the surface is only about 11
percent.

283
00:13:59,656 --> 00:14:02,100
The other 89 percent lies below the water.

284
00:14:02,100 --> 00:14:06,460
So a full ninety percent of any iceberg
lies down beneath the surface.

285
00:14:06,460 --> 00:14:08,396
So when you spot an iceberg from far away

286
00:14:08,396 --> 00:14:12,100
the ice beneath the water may already be
very close to you, without your ever
knowing it.

287
00:14:12,100 --> 00:14:12,443
Now,

288
00:14:12,443 --> 00:14:14,928
ice that floats on the ocean,

289
00:14:14,928 --> 00:14:17,585
up on the surface of the water,

290
00:14:17,585 --> 00:14:20,927
doesn't actually cause the sea to rise;

291
00:14:20,927 --> 00:14:25,470
it only leaves the polar bears with
nowhere to stand,

292
00:14:25,470 --> 00:14:32,069
because as the ice melts they may have to
travel very far to find a foothold.

293
00:14:32,100 --> 00:14:35,757
In other words, what really causes the sea
to rise

294
00:14:35,757 --> 00:14:38,253
is mainly the ice sitting on the land.

295
00:14:39,360 --> 00:14:41,640
And what do people usually mean by that?

296
00:14:41,640 --> 00:14:43,962
They mean Greenland, and Antarctica.

297
00:14:45,400 --> 00:14:47,040
Here's a news story for you.

298
00:14:47,040 --> 00:14:48,502
Just this past August

299
00:14:48,502 --> 00:14:50,061
you'd find it quite remarkable

300
00:14:50,061 --> 00:14:53,180
it rained in Greenland for the very first
time.

301
00:14:53,180 --> 00:14:55,563
At first I really didn't think much of it.

302
00:14:55,563 --> 00:14:57,766
My immediate reaction was something like
this:

303
00:14:57,766 --> 00:15:00,960
surely it must have rained in Greenland
before?

304
00:15:00,960 --> 00:15:03,572
Why do you think this made it into the
headlines?

305
00:15:04,960 --> 00:15:09,960
Well, just think about it: Greenland is to
the east of Canada, very far to the north.

306
00:15:09,960 --> 00:15:14,720
It really shouldn't be raining there at
all, it should be snowing, do you see?

307
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:20,960
So the first recorded rainfall in
Greenland shows that the temperature has
genuinely risen.

308
00:15:20,960 --> 00:15:25,082
And on your right, you can see those dogs
pulling along a sled

309
00:15:25,082 --> 00:15:29,319
they're not walking on water; it's that
the surface layer of the ice

310
00:15:29,319 --> 00:15:31,931
is melting very, very badly.

311
00:15:32,260 --> 00:15:33,875
So when you photograph it from this angle

312
00:15:33,875 --> 00:15:35,248
it looks as though the dogs

313
00:15:35,248 --> 00:15:37,712
are walking right across the surface of
the water.

314
00:15:37,712 --> 00:15:39,273
This is actually in Greenland

315
00:15:39,273 --> 00:15:40,886
that is to say, Greenland

316
00:15:40,886 --> 00:15:43,280
the melting of the surface ice there.

317
00:15:43,280 --> 00:15:47,150
Someone has calculated that if all of
Greenland's ice were to melt

318
00:15:47,150 --> 00:15:52,240
the sea would rise by around twenty-odd
feet, roughly two storeys high.

319
00:15:52,240 --> 00:15:52,916
Two storeys

320
00:15:52,916 --> 00:15:58,120
twenty-odd feet is really very high; I
wonder how close to the sea your homes
happen to be.

321
00:15:58,120 --> 00:15:59,170
I wonder for all of you

322
00:15:59,170 --> 00:16:00,503
which floor you live on.

323
00:16:00,503 --> 00:16:05,560
But what if the water, the ice of
Antarctica, were to melt, what then?

324
00:16:05,560 --> 00:16:09,360
Antarctica holds nine times as much ice as
Greenland does.

325
00:16:09,360 --> 00:16:15,223
That means if the ice of Antarctica and
the ice of Greenland were to melt together

326
00:16:15,223 --> 00:16:20,448
the world's sea level would rise up by
around 21 or 22 storeys.

327
00:16:21,760 --> 00:16:24,140
Would you like to picture that scene for a
moment?

328
00:16:24,160 --> 00:16:28,049
And this is only the rise caused by the
land ice melting.

329
00:16:30,160 --> 00:16:35,369
In fact, when the temperature rises, there
is another factor, another influence, that
is very significant.

330
00:16:35,369 --> 00:16:39,529
It's that, as the temperature climbs, the
seawater itself expands.

331
00:16:39,529 --> 00:16:45,160
And this expansion of the sea, which
raises the sea level, is actually a very
serious matter.

332
00:16:45,160 --> 00:16:47,830
This February there was a news story.

333
00:16:49,160 --> 00:16:53,060
For the very first time, a Russian
icebreaker was able to cross

334
00:16:53,060 --> 00:16:58,540
a passage in the Arctic Ocean that had
never once been navigable before.

335
00:16:58,540 --> 00:16:59,832
So what does this actually tell us?

336
00:16:59,832 --> 00:17:02,874
It tells us that in the Arctic Ocean, at
the North Pole

337
00:17:02,874 --> 00:17:05,440
the ice has grown very much thinner.

338
00:17:05,440 --> 00:17:06,477
Now you might well say

339
00:17:06,477 --> 00:17:08,975
hold on a moment, you've only just
finished telling us

340
00:17:08,975 --> 00:17:10,813
you said that at the North Pole

341
00:17:10,813 --> 00:17:14,160
ice melting on the sea won't affect the
height of the water.

342
00:17:14,160 --> 00:17:15,089
But here's the thing

343
00:17:15,089 --> 00:17:16,714
there are still other effects.

344
00:17:17,480 --> 00:17:20,800
In recent years, haven't you often been
hearing about heatwaves?

345
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:23,064
We went through one this June.

346
00:17:23,160 --> 00:17:28,280
This June a heatwave struck Canada, and
Canada is very far to the north.

347
00:17:28,280 --> 00:17:31,360
Vancouver sits at 50 degrees north
latitude.

348
00:17:31,360 --> 00:17:32,927
But just think of Vancouver's

349
00:17:32,927 --> 00:17:35,482
neighbour, the city of Calgary.

350
00:17:35,700 --> 00:17:36,745
In Calgary

351
00:17:36,745 --> 00:17:39,299
it reached over 40 degrees Celsius.

352
00:17:39,800 --> 00:17:41,840
You might well find that very strange.

353
00:17:41,840 --> 00:17:46,322
Why would there be a heatwave so far
north? If you look at the heatwaves of
recent years

354
00:17:46,322 --> 00:17:50,118
they're often caused by one thing, what's
called a "heat dome."

355
00:17:50,118 --> 00:17:52,840
In Chinese it's popularly known as a "heat
lid."

356
00:17:52,840 --> 00:17:54,617
a lid, as in a covering lid.

357
00:17:54,617 --> 00:17:57,720
Just imagine you're cooking something,
boiling some water.

358
00:17:57,720 --> 00:17:59,804
With a lid on, or without a lid

359
00:17:59,804 --> 00:18:00,780
isn't it different?

360
00:18:00,780 --> 00:18:03,688
With the lid on, doesn't it come to a boil
much faster?

361
00:18:03,688 --> 00:18:05,666
Because all of that steam simply can't
escape.

362
00:18:05,666 --> 00:18:06,555
The steam can't get out

363
00:18:06,555 --> 00:18:07,887
so it boils very quickly.

364
00:18:07,887 --> 00:18:09,503
It's exactly the same principle at work.

365
00:18:09,503 --> 00:18:10,842
Because in this picture, you can see

366
00:18:10,842 --> 00:18:13,073
can you see, there's a jet stream right
here

367
00:18:13,073 --> 00:18:16,400
that is, a high-speed air current, way up
high in the atmosphere.

368
00:18:16,400 --> 00:18:17,773
It forms an area of high pressure

369
00:18:17,773 --> 00:18:20,156
so that all the air trapped down beneath
it

370
00:18:20,156 --> 00:18:22,336
even though it isn't hot in itself

371
00:18:22,336 --> 00:18:23,386
simply cannot disperse.

372
00:18:23,386 --> 00:18:24,880
It has no way at all to escape.

373
00:18:24,880 --> 00:18:26,455
It's just like putting a lid on the pot.

374
00:18:26,455 --> 00:18:28,272
So that within a very short span of time

375
00:18:28,272 --> 00:18:29,322
all of that trapped air

376
00:18:29,322 --> 00:18:30,695
heats up extremely fast.

377
00:18:30,695 --> 00:18:33,078
So just think about it, at over 50 degrees
north

378
00:18:33,078 --> 00:18:35,461
you can still record over 40 degrees
Celsius.

379
00:18:35,461 --> 00:18:37,035
That really is quite staggering.

380
00:18:37,035 --> 00:18:37,439
Alright.

381
00:18:37,439 --> 00:18:39,095
But there's something I want you to
notice.

382
00:18:39,095 --> 00:18:41,276
I'm not really here just to introduce the
heat dome.

383
00:18:41,276 --> 00:18:43,376
Let me add one more term while we're at
it.

384
00:18:43,376 --> 00:18:44,950
This kind of air-current phenomenon

385
00:18:44,950 --> 00:18:46,889
is actually called an "Omega Block."

386
00:18:46,889 --> 00:18:51,400
because its shape closely resembles the
Greek letter Omega.

387
00:18:51,400 --> 00:18:56,508
What I really want you to notice is the
influence of the high-altitude jet stream.

388
00:18:57,400 --> 00:18:59,954
You'll find that because of the way it
wanders

389
00:18:59,954 --> 00:19:03,902
hot air gets trapped, and so you get heat
domes, and heatwaves.

390
00:19:04,400 --> 00:19:07,262
And likewise, because of this very same
wandering

391
00:19:07,262 --> 00:19:10,068
you'll see it drives the cold air out of
the Arctic

392
00:19:10,068 --> 00:19:14,221
sending those polar vortexes down toward
the south

393
00:19:14,221 --> 00:19:17,700
reaching much further south into some
regions.

394
00:19:17,700 --> 00:19:21,197
So in recent years you constantly hear
news of

395
00:19:21,197 --> 00:19:23,899
many places suffering from heatwaves

396
00:19:23,899 --> 00:19:25,329
while at the very same time

397
00:19:25,329 --> 00:19:26,389
many other places

398
00:19:26,389 --> 00:19:28,720
are hitting record-breaking cold.

399
00:19:28,720 --> 00:19:29,913
There was even a time

400
00:19:29,913 --> 00:19:31,262
on one occasion

401
00:19:31,262 --> 00:19:32,040
when Toronto

402
00:19:32,040 --> 00:19:35,360
recorded a ground temperature colder than
the Arctic itself.

403
00:19:35,360 --> 00:19:36,640
And why is that?

404
00:19:36,640 --> 00:19:38,960
It's because of this very phenomenon.

405
00:19:38,960 --> 00:19:40,575
You might want to ask a question here.

406
00:19:40,575 --> 00:19:43,820
Why does this jet stream wander about like
this?

407
00:19:43,820 --> 00:19:46,084
Well, the reason the jet stream wanders

408
00:19:46,084 --> 00:19:48,696
is that it has grown weaker.

409
00:19:49,460 --> 00:19:52,520
Because it has weakened, because it has
slowed right down.

410
00:19:52,520 --> 00:19:54,320
Just imagine it, it's like a river.

411
00:19:54,320 --> 00:19:57,268
when it reaches its lower course, when it
reaches the flat plains

412
00:19:57,268 --> 00:20:00,175
doesn't it slow right down? Because the
slope is no longer as steep.

413
00:20:00,175 --> 00:20:03,123
And in order to keep moving forward,
doesn't it begin to wind about?

414
00:20:03,123 --> 00:20:04,981
That river starts to meander, doesn't it?

415
00:20:04,981 --> 00:20:07,040
Well, it's exactly the same situation
here.

416
00:20:07,040 --> 00:20:08,260
Because the jet stream

417
00:20:08,260 --> 00:20:09,943
has weakened and slowed down

418
00:20:09,943 --> 00:20:13,542
it begins to wander; and because of this
wandering

419
00:20:13,542 --> 00:20:21,379
the heatwaves I spoke of earlier, and
those invasions of Arctic cold, are
becoming more and more frequent.

420
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:26,528
Alright, so now you'll ask a question: why
has it grown weaker?

421
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:32,000
Well, this actually proves one thing: that
the Arctic has warmed a great deal.

422
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:35,957
Because the difference between the
Arctic's temperature and that of the
surrounding regions

423
00:20:35,957 --> 00:20:42,055
the greater that difference is, the faster
the surrounding air rushes in toward the
Arctic, and the stronger the jet stream
becomes.

424
00:20:42,055 --> 00:20:49,253
But once the Arctic warms up, that
temperature gap shrinks, and so this jet
stream grows weaker.

425
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:52,473
In fact,

426
00:20:52,473 --> 00:20:56,438
research shows that global temperature
change isn't uniform at all,

427
00:20:56,438 --> 00:21:02,000
and in the Arctic the temperature is
rising four times faster than in the
surrounding regions.

428
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:05,069
In other words, the Arctic region is
warming up far more quickly.

429
00:21:05,069 --> 00:21:10,000
You might want to ask a question, namely:
why is it warming so very fast?

430
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:14,100
One of the main reasons is the great
Siberian wildfires.

431
00:21:14,100 --> 00:21:19,683
This fire has actually been burning for
several years now; and in fact, the
largest fire of the entire 21st century

432
00:21:19,683 --> 00:21:23,320
is the great blaze burning this year in
Siberia, in Russia.

433
00:21:23,320 --> 00:21:27,666
The area of this one fire, compared with
North America's

434
00:21:27,666 --> 00:21:30,350
the wildfires of California and Canada put
together

435
00:21:30,350 --> 00:21:35,080
add to that the fires of Europe, and of
Turkey, and this one is still larger in
area.

436
00:21:35,080 --> 00:21:36,976
This was captured from high up above

437
00:21:36,976 --> 00:21:43,280
the situation of the Siberian fires; and
you'll notice, aren't there many spots
where there's no smoke at all?

438
00:21:43,280 --> 00:21:47,380
Why is it so strange? Why can I see only
smoke, and no actual flames?

439
00:21:47,380 --> 00:21:53,020
It turns out the fires in Siberia, within
the Arctic Circle, are very peculiar
indeed.

440
00:21:53,020 --> 00:21:58,380
This kind of fire has a name: it's called
a "zombie fire." And as the name suggests,
do you know what it means?

441
00:21:58,380 --> 00:22:01,200
You can't smother it, and you can't put it
out.

442
00:22:01,200 --> 00:22:04,929
Why is that? Because inside the Arctic
Circle it's so cold

443
00:22:04,929 --> 00:22:06,719
that after many of the plants die

444
00:22:06,719 --> 00:22:08,360
they don't decompose.

445
00:22:08,360 --> 00:22:11,700
So beneath the soil there's a great deal
of organic matter.

446
00:22:11,700 --> 00:22:14,260
And when a fire breaks out, sometimes
these flames

447
00:22:14,260 --> 00:22:16,300
will burn their way down underground.

448
00:22:16,300 --> 00:22:18,106
And once they've burned down underground

449
00:22:18,106 --> 00:22:18,678
you'll find that

450
00:22:18,678 --> 00:22:20,660
you simply cannot put them out.

451
00:22:20,660 --> 00:22:23,333
But given the chance, they'll burn their
way back up again

452
00:22:23,333 --> 00:22:24,500
to the surface.

453
00:22:24,500 --> 00:22:27,560
So this fire has, in fact, been burning
for several years now.

454
00:22:27,560 --> 00:22:29,095
Does it have any consequences?

455
00:22:29,095 --> 00:22:32,926
This here is the ground, captured in
satellite imagery.

456
00:22:32,940 --> 00:22:35,040
You can see all those points of fire,
can't you?

457
00:22:35,040 --> 00:22:37,340
Underground, it's still burning away.

458
00:22:37,340 --> 00:22:40,126
But the moment it gets a chance, it breaks
right out to the surface.

459
00:22:40,126 --> 00:22:44,000
The whole landscape smoking over, that's
exactly what's happening here.

460
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,060
You might ask, the great Siberian fires

461
00:22:46,060 --> 00:22:48,886
these Arctic Circle fires, what impact do
they really have?

462
00:22:48,886 --> 00:22:51,188
Last year there was a news story just like
this.

463
00:22:51,188 --> 00:22:55,520
You see the picture at the top, that
river, has it not turned completely red?

464
00:22:55,520 --> 00:22:57,320
And it's not because of any blood.

465
00:22:57,820 --> 00:22:58,807
It's rather because of

466
00:22:58,807 --> 00:22:59,736
an oil spill.

467
00:23:00,640 --> 00:23:02,633
You see, the ground in the Arctic Circle

468
00:23:02,633 --> 00:23:07,680
is called permafrost, ground that has
built up a great deal of ice and frozen
solid.

469
00:23:07,680 --> 00:23:11,759
And on top of it sit many oil refineries,
and many storage tanks.

470
00:23:11,759 --> 00:23:15,486
But as the Arctic Circle warms and this
ice melts away

471
00:23:15,486 --> 00:23:21,680
many of these facilities, the storage
tanks for instance, subside, and start
leaking oil.

472
00:23:21,680 --> 00:23:27,775
This was the spill back in May of last
year, and it was a genuine ecological
disaster.

473
00:23:28,680 --> 00:23:33,963
If you go to Siberia these days, you'll
often see craters just like these.

474
00:23:34,320 --> 00:23:39,680
Looking at the people standing beside
them, you can grasp just how huge these
craters are.

475
00:23:39,680 --> 00:23:42,000
So why are there such enormous craters?

476
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:46,340
Who would have the time to dig such holes?
In fact, they weren't dug out at all.

477
00:23:46,340 --> 00:23:49,823
It's because the permafrost has warmed up
and the ice has melted

478
00:23:49,823 --> 00:23:54,235
releasing the methane that had been
trapped down in the ground.

479
00:23:57,360 --> 00:23:58,637
And this methane

480
00:23:58,637 --> 00:23:59,450
here you can see

481
00:23:59,450 --> 00:24:01,423
it's rather like a bubble.

482
00:24:02,160 --> 00:24:03,860
That is, down beneath the soil

483
00:24:03,860 --> 00:24:05,610
beneath all that vegetation

484
00:24:05,610 --> 00:24:07,560
there's a pocket of methane gas.

485
00:24:07,560 --> 00:24:09,703
And sometimes it simply explodes

486
00:24:09,703 --> 00:24:12,560
which created those very craters you saw a
moment ago.

487
00:24:12,560 --> 00:24:16,160
And those bubbles down beneath the water's
surface

488
00:24:16,160 --> 00:24:18,160
are all of them methane.

489
00:24:18,160 --> 00:24:19,993
If you go and find some of the photos
online

490
00:24:19,993 --> 00:24:21,285
there are people playing around with it

491
00:24:21,285 --> 00:24:22,285
right there on the ice

492
00:24:22,285 --> 00:24:25,160
they bore a hole into it, and then set it
alight.

493
00:24:25,160 --> 00:24:26,321
Now this methane

494
00:24:26,321 --> 00:24:27,772
as a greenhouse gas

495
00:24:27,772 --> 00:24:32,881
methane's effect is actually 20 times
greater than that of carbon dioxide.

496
00:24:35,300 --> 00:24:39,623
In other words, take a given volume of
carbon dioxide and its effect on
temperature

497
00:24:39,623 --> 00:24:43,480
swap it out for methane, and its effect is
a full twenty times as strong.

498
00:24:43,480 --> 00:24:45,136
So someone has actually studied this

499
00:24:45,136 --> 00:24:46,226
and what did he say?

500
00:24:46,226 --> 00:24:49,709
He said that once Siberia's methane is
released

501
00:24:49,709 --> 00:24:55,456
the impact would equal that of all the
world's carbon dioxide emissions combined.

502
00:24:57,360 --> 00:25:00,204
That is an enormous crisis indeed.

503
00:25:00,520 --> 00:25:02,871
This also brings to my mind, in the Bible

504
00:25:02,871 --> 00:25:04,020
another verse.

505
00:25:04,020 --> 00:25:08,328
In the Book of Revelation, speaking of the
days of disaster still to come

506
00:25:08,328 --> 00:25:10,310
an angel pours out his bowl upon the sun

507
00:25:10,310 --> 00:25:13,240
and the sun is given power to scorch
people with fire.

508
00:25:13,240 --> 00:25:18,431
Already, some places are hitting over 40
degrees; I mentioned Vancouver just now

509
00:25:18,431 --> 00:25:19,640
and Calgary.

510
00:25:19,640 --> 00:25:23,181
And some places have even reached over 50
degrees.

511
00:25:23,980 --> 00:25:27,463
As I was saying, if this methane were to
be released in that way

512
00:25:27,463 --> 00:25:30,598
then for the temperature of the whole
planet

513
00:25:30,598 --> 00:25:32,804
the effect could come on very quickly

514
00:25:32,804 --> 00:25:34,313
very abruptly indeed.

515
00:25:36,060 --> 00:25:37,660
So if you were to ask me

516
00:25:37,660 --> 00:25:40,425
this idea of the sun scorching people

517
00:25:40,425 --> 00:25:41,298
I don't think it is

518
00:25:41,298 --> 00:25:43,480
some far-fetched fairy tale at all.

519
00:25:43,480 --> 00:25:46,673
In fact, there are already signs now
showing us

520
00:25:46,673 --> 00:25:48,182
that this global warming

521
00:25:48,182 --> 00:25:48,937
can indeed

522
00:25:48,937 --> 00:25:50,156
turn very fast

523
00:25:50,156 --> 00:25:51,897
and grow very severe.

524
00:25:53,480 --> 00:25:55,330
In this picture here, it lets us see

525
00:25:55,330 --> 00:25:57,780
the situation of the great forest fires.

526
00:25:57,780 --> 00:26:00,780
We've already talked about the Siberian
blaze.

527
00:26:00,780 --> 00:26:01,830
You can see it right here.

528
00:26:01,830 --> 00:26:03,780
And here you can also see several more
fires.

529
00:26:03,780 --> 00:26:06,218
This one here is the Amazon rainforest.

530
00:26:06,780 --> 00:26:09,780
This one is the Congo Basin, in Africa.

531
00:26:09,780 --> 00:26:12,041
And this one is Borneo, in Indonesia.

532
00:26:12,041 --> 00:26:13,340
All of them are tropical regions

533
00:26:13,340 --> 00:26:15,695
all of them are tropical rainforests

534
00:26:15,695 --> 00:26:16,547
rainforest zones

535
00:26:16,547 --> 00:26:18,780
and all of them are burning very severely.

536
00:26:18,780 --> 00:26:21,780
We've already spoken about the Siberian
fire.

537
00:26:21,780 --> 00:26:23,780
But what about the Amazon rainforest?

538
00:26:23,780 --> 00:26:28,308
In fact, many of the Amazon fires are
deliberately set by people.

539
00:26:29,420 --> 00:26:33,367
Some of them are for felling the trees, to
take the timber.

540
00:26:33,760 --> 00:26:37,600
But more often, they burn down the forest
afterward in order to raise cattle.

541
00:26:37,600 --> 00:26:38,620
And why is that?

542
00:26:38,620 --> 00:26:42,980
Because the market price of cattle, the
value of the cattle, is high.

543
00:26:42,980 --> 00:26:45,120
They can be sold off for a good deal more
money.

544
00:26:45,120 --> 00:26:48,438
So you'll notice that one of the themes at
the climate summit

545
00:26:48,438 --> 00:26:51,119
was to offer subsidies to certain
countries

546
00:26:51,119 --> 00:26:53,028
countries such as Brazil, for example

547
00:26:53,028 --> 00:26:56,800
so that it would ban people from clearing
the Amazon rainforest.

548
00:26:56,800 --> 00:26:57,567
But the truth is

549
00:26:57,567 --> 00:27:00,556
I'm not sure whether by now it might
already be a little too late.

550
00:27:00,556 --> 00:27:01,202
And why is that?

551
00:27:01,202 --> 00:27:03,347
In recent years many studies

552
00:27:03,347 --> 00:27:06,119
have shown the Amazon to be the world's
very largest

553
00:27:06,119 --> 00:27:08,107
tropical rainforest zone

554
00:27:08,107 --> 00:27:10,880
you could even call it the earth's first
lung.

555
00:27:10,880 --> 00:27:11,925
But here's the thing

556
00:27:11,925 --> 00:27:13,260
some studies show

557
00:27:13,260 --> 00:27:14,827
that the Amazon's carbon emissions

558
00:27:14,827 --> 00:27:17,788
have now surpassed its carbon absorption.

559
00:27:20,340 --> 00:27:21,394
Generally speaking

560
00:27:21,394 --> 00:27:22,181
a rainforest,

561
00:27:22,181 --> 00:27:24,665
when there's sunlight and photosynthesis,

562
00:27:24,665 --> 00:27:30,116
draws in the carbon dioxide from the
atmosphere and turns it into the carbon of
its trees;

563
00:27:30,116 --> 00:27:32,720
and that is what we call carbon
absorption.

564
00:27:32,720 --> 00:27:34,824
But why are the emissions now greater?

565
00:27:34,824 --> 00:27:40,194
Well, for one thing, when it catches fire,
doesn't it release enormous amounts of
carbon dioxide?

566
00:27:40,194 --> 00:27:43,018
And it also emits a great many carbon
particulates.

567
00:27:43,018 --> 00:27:46,371
The trend over these last few years is
that

568
00:27:46,371 --> 00:27:52,140
the Amazon's carbon emissions have already
exceeded its carbon absorption.

569
00:27:52,140 --> 00:28:00,093
And it makes me think, these days, isn't
there always this coronavirus around? And
the problem it causes is pneumonia.

570
00:28:01,140 --> 00:28:06,140
Pneumonia means you can't breathe; it's as
if the earth itself has come down with
pneumonia.

571
00:28:06,140 --> 00:28:13,106
Then there's also the Congo Basin in
Africa that I mentioned earlier.

572
00:28:13,140 --> 00:28:15,087
This one you could call the world's second
lung

573
00:28:15,087 --> 00:28:18,140
and it, too, is a tropical rainforest
zone.

574
00:28:18,140 --> 00:28:19,896
In the two photos over on your right

575
00:28:19,896 --> 00:28:24,140
you can see that the firefighting
equipment there is really quite primitive.

576
00:28:24,140 --> 00:28:26,321
How do you even fight a great fire like
this?

577
00:28:26,321 --> 00:28:28,582
Just with those things, spraying a little
here, a little there.

578
00:28:28,582 --> 00:28:31,247
Like a water pistol, how could that ever
put it out?

579
00:28:31,247 --> 00:28:33,549
But how did these fires actually start?

580
00:28:33,549 --> 00:28:35,851
In fact, they were set deliberately by
people.

581
00:28:35,851 --> 00:28:37,142
Because over in Africa

582
00:28:37,142 --> 00:28:38,939
in order to get food, you have to farm

583
00:28:38,939 --> 00:28:41,060
and how do they go about farming?

584
00:28:41,060 --> 00:28:44,120
They use a method that's called
"slash-and-burn."

585
00:28:44,120 --> 00:28:46,180
That is to say, they burn down a patch of
forest

586
00:28:46,180 --> 00:28:48,360
and that gives them a clearing to work
with.

587
00:28:48,360 --> 00:28:51,793
The dead leaves even act like a fertiliser
to help the crops along.

588
00:28:51,793 --> 00:28:53,731
And then they plant their crops right
there.

589
00:28:53,731 --> 00:28:55,387
And after they've farmed it for a few
years

590
00:28:55,387 --> 00:28:58,254
that ground becomes overrun with a great
many weeds

591
00:28:58,254 --> 00:29:01,848
so the people move off elsewhere, and burn
down yet another patch.

592
00:29:01,848 --> 00:29:03,307
And the plot they'd farmed first

593
00:29:03,307 --> 00:29:04,059
is simply left

594
00:29:04,059 --> 00:29:07,420
to slowly recover, and grow all its plants
back again.

595
00:29:07,420 --> 00:29:10,740
This is actually how it has always worked,
all along.

596
00:29:10,740 --> 00:29:12,740
This slash-and-burn method of working

597
00:29:12,740 --> 00:29:15,100
is called "slash-and-burn cultivation."

598
00:29:15,100 --> 00:29:18,209
So why has it now spiralled so completely
out of control?

599
00:29:18,209 --> 00:29:20,592
This actually leads us on to another
problem.

600
00:29:20,592 --> 00:29:22,005
Because there are more people now.

601
00:29:22,005 --> 00:29:24,179
Because the population has grown so much

602
00:29:24,179 --> 00:29:25,777
those abandoned plots have no time

603
00:29:25,777 --> 00:29:27,640
not enough time left to recover.

604
00:29:27,640 --> 00:29:30,110
Because there are so many more mouths to
feed

605
00:29:30,110 --> 00:29:32,095
they have no choice but to keep on burning

606
00:29:32,095 --> 00:29:35,580
and so the forests are given no time at
all to recover.

607
00:29:35,580 --> 00:29:40,180
And so the world's second lung has come
down with pneumonia as well.

608
00:29:40,180 --> 00:29:42,500
And this brings us on to the second
problem.

609
00:29:42,500 --> 00:29:44,761
And it's that over these last hundred
years or so

610
00:29:44,761 --> 00:29:48,355
the world's population has grown at a
breakneck speed.

611
00:29:48,355 --> 00:29:50,387
As you can see here from this chart

612
00:29:50,387 --> 00:29:53,754
in 1930 the whole world had 2 billion
people.

613
00:29:54,020 --> 00:29:56,503
But by this year, and it's already late
November now

614
00:29:56,503 --> 00:29:59,640
the global population has reached 7.9
billion.

615
00:29:59,640 --> 00:30:02,717
According to the projections, in spite of
the pandemic

616
00:30:02,717 --> 00:30:05,039
by the end of next year, perhaps

617
00:30:05,039 --> 00:30:09,450
the world's population will break right
past 8 billion.

618
00:30:09,620 --> 00:30:11,807
That is to say, going from 2 billion

619
00:30:11,807 --> 00:30:12,977
all the way to 8 billion

620
00:30:12,977 --> 00:30:15,520
in less than a hundred years, all told.

621
00:30:15,520 --> 00:30:18,306
This is a very serious problem indeed.

622
00:30:18,580 --> 00:30:20,811
Because when the population grows so large

623
00:30:20,811 --> 00:30:22,940
the earth's resources are limited.

624
00:30:22,940 --> 00:30:26,897
And which regions are seeing the
population grow most explosively?

625
00:30:26,897 --> 00:30:27,800
It's in Africa.

626
00:30:27,800 --> 00:30:30,047
So some people have even estimated

627
00:30:30,047 --> 00:30:31,451
that within a few years

628
00:30:31,451 --> 00:30:35,314
the world's largest city will not be New
York,

629
00:30:35,314 --> 00:30:37,077
nor will it be Tokyo,

630
00:30:37,077 --> 00:30:40,100
but a place in Nigeria called Lagos.

631
00:30:40,100 --> 00:30:47,260
Africa is where the population is growing
the fastest, and then there's the Middle
East region too.

632
00:30:47,260 --> 00:30:54,180
And there's India as well, of course; and
China's 1.3 billion are actually ageing
very rapidly indeed.

633
00:30:54,180 --> 00:31:02,180
That's why in recent years they've relaxed
the one-child policy, encouraging, even
urging people to have two or three
children.

634
00:31:02,180 --> 00:31:06,180
When there are so very many people, some
things simply can't be helped.

635
00:31:06,180 --> 00:31:10,180
So you'll notice that the climate summit
didn't even touch upon this issue.

636
00:31:10,180 --> 00:31:10,747
Instead,

637
00:31:10,747 --> 00:31:14,294
it leaned entirely on the carbon-emitting
nations;

638
00:31:14,294 --> 00:31:16,847
the biggest emitter of all is China,

639
00:31:16,847 --> 00:31:18,478
and the second is India

640
00:31:18,478 --> 00:31:21,180
both of them countries with well over a
billion people.

641
00:31:21,180 --> 00:31:25,011
But you know full well that these promises
are only words on paper.

642
00:31:25,380 --> 00:31:28,701
Why is that? Because just last month, for
instance

643
00:31:28,701 --> 00:31:33,220
you saw China facing a power shortage up
in the northeast, didn't you?

644
00:31:33,220 --> 00:31:36,531
Winter comes around once again, and once
again there's no electricity.

645
00:31:36,531 --> 00:31:40,166
And no one even knows where that heating
is supposed to come from, or how.

646
00:31:40,166 --> 00:31:44,002
And in China, the power is very often
generated by burning coal.

647
00:31:44,002 --> 00:31:48,040
The population is enormous, and there's a
power crunch on top of it.

648
00:31:48,040 --> 00:31:51,231
Not to mention that all of that
manufacturing needs electricity too.

649
00:31:51,231 --> 00:31:53,654
And the power comes mainly from the
burning of coal.

650
00:31:53,654 --> 00:31:56,763
So how can it possibly promise to cut its
emissions?

651
00:31:56,763 --> 00:31:57,369
So really

652
00:31:57,369 --> 00:32:00,801
this is a very big problem indeed; and in
the picture on the right

653
00:32:00,801 --> 00:32:05,364
many people are now concerned with what's
called the earth's carrying capacity.

654
00:32:05,364 --> 00:32:07,464
That is to say, just how many people can
the earth

655
00:32:07,464 --> 00:32:11,220
with its land area, and its resources,
actually manage to support?

656
00:32:11,220 --> 00:32:13,764
Over these past few decades many people
have studied this

657
00:32:13,764 --> 00:32:17,923
and most scientists share one consensus,
which is this: that the world's

658
00:32:17,923 --> 00:32:20,629
resources can supply around 8 billion
people.

659
00:32:20,629 --> 00:32:24,385
But don't forget, with all of these
natural disasters and this shifting
climate

660
00:32:24,385 --> 00:32:26,283
and I haven't even mentioned drought today

661
00:32:26,283 --> 00:32:28,948
you can imagine that the earth is really a
kind of balance.

662
00:32:28,948 --> 00:32:31,819
When some places are being drenched with
such heavy rain

663
00:32:31,819 --> 00:32:35,980
then you can well imagine that other
places are suffering the most extreme
drought.

664
00:32:35,980 --> 00:32:37,676
California is one such example.

665
00:32:37,676 --> 00:32:42,920
And these disasters, at the very same
time, lower the earth's carrying capacity.

666
00:32:42,920 --> 00:32:47,180
So really, this is a problem that no one
even knows how to solve.

667
00:32:47,180 --> 00:32:55,111
And so, speaking of all this, I'm reminded
of a certain ship, the Titanic; the
Titanic sank because it struck an iceberg.

668
00:32:55,111 --> 00:32:58,585
The story goes that at the moment it hit
the iceberg,

669
00:32:58,585 --> 00:33:01,272
the captain immediately called a meeting,

670
00:33:01,272 --> 00:33:04,812
and because the ship's designer happened
to be aboard,

671
00:33:04,812 --> 00:33:05,992
and the owner too,

672
00:33:05,992 --> 00:33:07,696
they all gathered together

673
00:33:07,696 --> 00:33:09,308
and after inspecting it,

674
00:33:09,308 --> 00:33:11,456
they discovered that at the bow,

675
00:33:11,456 --> 00:33:12,128
the first,

676
00:33:12,128 --> 00:33:12,867
the second,

677
00:33:12,867 --> 00:33:13,538
the third,

678
00:33:13,538 --> 00:33:14,277
the fourth,

679
00:33:14,277 --> 00:33:16,023
and the fifth compartment,

680
00:33:16,023 --> 00:33:16,560
that is,

681
00:33:16,560 --> 00:33:17,701
the ship's holds,

682
00:33:17,701 --> 00:33:19,380
were all taking on water.

683
00:33:19,380 --> 00:33:21,346
And that designer grew very anxious indeed

684
00:33:21,346 --> 00:33:23,480
because he was the one who had designed
the ship.

685
00:33:23,480 --> 00:33:24,571
So the captain asked

686
00:33:24,571 --> 00:33:28,080
this water coming in, can it be pumped
back out with the pumps?

687
00:33:28,080 --> 00:33:32,037
Everyone looked at one another, and they
already knew exactly what was about to
happen.

688
00:33:32,037 --> 00:33:34,592
Pumping out the water could only buy them
time

689
00:33:34,592 --> 00:33:36,159
just a few minutes, that's all.

690
00:33:36,280 --> 00:33:37,088
And why was that?

691
00:33:37,088 --> 00:33:37,779
Because this ship

692
00:33:37,779 --> 00:33:40,829
with three compartments, or four, flooded,
could still stay afloat

693
00:33:40,829 --> 00:33:43,960
but with five compartments gone, there was
no way it could stay up.

694
00:33:43,960 --> 00:33:45,781
So the ship's owner asked

695
00:33:45,781 --> 00:33:48,320
the owner said, how can this possibly be?

696
00:33:48,320 --> 00:33:50,904
This is her maiden voyage, her very first
one.

697
00:33:50,904 --> 00:33:52,633
And that designer answered him

698
00:33:52,633 --> 00:33:54,006
this ship is made of steel

699
00:33:54,006 --> 00:33:55,480
and she will sink.

700
00:33:55,480 --> 00:33:57,628
It's just a mathematical certainty

701
00:33:57,628 --> 00:34:02,330
that is to say, it is a mathematical
inevitability.

702
00:34:02,660 --> 00:34:04,767
And when you look at everything we've just
seen

703
00:34:04,767 --> 00:34:07,000
it really is a very hard thing to face.

704
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:09,247
It reminds me of a few years back,

705
00:34:09,247 --> 00:34:10,106
when Al Gore,

706
00:34:10,106 --> 00:34:13,279
after he'd left office as the US Vice
President,

707
00:34:13,279 --> 00:34:16,253
turned his attention to global climate
change

708
00:34:16,253 --> 00:34:20,240
and made a film, called An Inconvenient
Truth.

709
00:34:20,240 --> 00:34:24,117
What was the Hong Kong Chinese title
again? "The Truth of Despair."

710
00:34:24,117 --> 00:34:27,600
And I feel that, up to this point, there
really is no hope at all.

711
00:34:27,600 --> 00:34:32,760
Perhaps you'll say, the whole world can
join hands and cut its carbon emissions.

712
00:34:32,760 --> 00:34:36,641
But as I mentioned earlier, at the very
least it overlooks one enormous problem

713
00:34:36,641 --> 00:34:38,960
and that is the problem of population.

714
00:34:38,960 --> 00:34:44,169
And getting the nations to take turns
cutting emissions together is itself
another problem.

715
00:34:44,169 --> 00:34:47,276
So this is exactly why seventy percent of
young people

716
00:34:47,276 --> 00:34:49,229
have so little hope for what lies ahead

717
00:34:49,229 --> 00:34:50,960
and are utterly filled with fear.

718
00:34:50,960 --> 00:34:52,715
And thinking it all over, I feel

719
00:34:52,715 --> 00:34:54,552
that if there really were no hope at all

720
00:34:54,552 --> 00:34:56,960
I honestly wouldn't know what on earth to
share with you.

721
00:34:56,960 --> 00:34:58,960
But here is something precious.

722
00:34:58,960 --> 00:35:01,687
At its very end, the Bible leaves us with
this one saying

723
00:35:01,687 --> 00:35:03,960
and it's spoken by the Lord Jesus Himself.

724
00:35:03,960 --> 00:35:05,656
The Lord Jesus, He sent His angel

725
00:35:05,656 --> 00:35:08,725
to testify these things to the churches,
and what did He say?

726
00:35:08,725 --> 00:35:11,350
There's a line that follows; what is it
that He says?

727
00:35:11,350 --> 00:35:13,369
He says: I am the bright morning star.

728
00:35:13,369 --> 00:35:16,963
You see, the deeper the night grows, the
darker and the colder it becomes.

729
00:35:16,963 --> 00:35:20,388
And at the coldest hour of all, just
before the dawn

730
00:35:20,388 --> 00:35:22,478
the morning star appears.

731
00:35:22,960 --> 00:35:26,960
And when people see the morning star, they
know the dawn is very near.

732
00:35:26,960 --> 00:35:28,818
So what does the Lord mean by this?

733
00:35:28,960 --> 00:35:31,514
He means to give people hope.

734
00:35:31,960 --> 00:35:33,121
That when you see Him

735
00:35:33,121 --> 00:35:34,979
the dawn is close at hand.

736
00:35:36,780 --> 00:35:37,800
And why is that?

737
00:35:37,800 --> 00:35:41,380
Because the Lord Jesus has already
promised to prepare a place for us.

738
00:35:41,380 --> 00:35:47,520
And once He has prepared that place, He
will come again to receive all who believe
unto Himself.

739
00:35:47,520 --> 00:35:49,229
So the state of this whole world

740
00:35:49,229 --> 00:35:52,317
is really meant to prepare people's hearts

741
00:35:52,317 --> 00:35:53,254
to let people know

742
00:35:53,254 --> 00:35:55,460
that in the world there is no hope.

743
00:35:55,460 --> 00:35:56,885
But how precious it is

744
00:35:56,885 --> 00:35:58,361
that those who have the Lord Jesus

745
00:35:58,361 --> 00:36:01,720
they have a living hope, which is that the
Lord Jesus is coming back

746
00:36:01,720 --> 00:36:03,960
to receive us unto Himself.

747
00:36:03,960 --> 00:36:07,037
And now, I'd like to share a hymn with you
all.

748
00:36:08,560 --> 00:36:11,669
This hymn is called "The Lord, Like the
Bright Morning Star."

749
00:36:11,669 --> 00:36:14,052
It brings to us hope, it brings to us
light

750
00:36:14,052 --> 00:36:18,292
so that even in our despair we can still
see the way out, far in the distance.

